;; talk/operator--protocol.scm — objections to surfaces/operator.scm, lens: protocol.

(objection 'o-operator-protocol-1 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'operator-check-flow :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/entity/scheduler/dispute-deadline-hook.ts" "The flow ends with 'nothing needs me, close this', but every deadline the hub keeps (closing a customer's exit case, answering it, filling paid requests) runs on a timer inside the hub's own runtime, and the list of what needs attention is built by that same runtime (operator-needs-attention). If the hub process is stopped, stuck or cut off from the chain, the list is empty or stale at exactly the moment deadlines are being missed, and the surface would read as all clear. operator-needs-wake does not say the alert comes from outside the hub process. The cost is an operator who closes the screen while a customer's exit window runs out with nobody answering for the hub."))

(objection 'o-operator-protocol-2 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'operator-default-close-cases :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/entity/tx/handlers/dispute/finalize-proof.ts" "Risk is marked 'nobody', but closing a case is the step that decides which signed state the chain pays out. The hub's closing picks a newer signed state only if one is already stored on the account; otherwise it closes on the state the customer started with ('initialNonce (unilateral)'). So a customer who starts from an old state in their favour is paid from it when the hub closes without asking, and the difference is the hub's loss. d-operator-disputes admits it did not verify that a newer state still wins on a plain account, and operator-leaving-flow promises to show 'whether it was in time'. The cost is a hub operator told that automatic closing endangers no one, when the holder carries the loss."))

(objection 'o-operator-protocol-3 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'operator-default-own-top-up :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/account/consensus/helpers.ts" "The default says the hub's side starts with the automatic request for more locked money switched on. The runtime states that automatic requests are driven by the customer's side and 'the hub never initiates it': the post-frame check returns nothing when the owning entity is a hub, and the policy handler skips it for a hub too. The setting exists on the hub's side but nothing ever acts on it. An operator reading it will believe what customers owe without collateral gets backed up by itself, while it only grows until someone acts; the cost is unsecured exposure the operator thought was being reduced."))

(objection 'o-operator-protocol-4 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'operator-prices-flow :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/entity/tx/handlers/account/lifecycle/admin.ts" "Signing hub-terms replaces the hub's whole fee and dispute configuration, it does not change one value: any field left out falls back to its default (forwarding fee 1 millionth, base fee 0, swap fee 0, collateral fee 1 hundredth of a percent, ten-minute refund timeout, automatic closing of exit cases) and the result is sent to every account. An operator who raises the swap fee alone resets a collateral fee they had set higher, or switches back to automatic closing a hub they had set to ignore, for every customer at once. The flow's promise to show which charges went up and which went down says nothing of the charges that moved because they were not named. Also, top-up-base-fee and top-up-chain-fee are shown as things the operator charges, but hub-terms sends the token default for the first and zero for the second, so the operator cannot set them here."))

(objection 'o-operator-protocol-5 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'operator-default-notice :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/account/config/term-notice.ts" "The default says a fee rise waits thirty days before it applies to existing customers, and operator-prices-flow promises 'the date a rise starts applying'. The notice logic compares only the flat collateral fee, the chain-cost charge and the collateral fee rate (and a shortened notice period). The forwarding fee and the swap fee are not announced on any account and have no waiting period, so a rise in them takes effect for the next payment or swap. The operator is told every rise has a start date, and the customers, who can only protect themselves by a payment cap per payment, are not given one for the charges that matter most to hub traffic."))

(objection 'o-operator-protocol-6 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'operator-earnings-flow :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/entity/scheduler/rebalance.ts" "The flow signs a withdrawal and a chain submission and ends by saying what stays on the chain still covers the paid requests waiting. The hub has one chain batch in flight at a time: while it is pending the hub's automation skips filling paid requests ('R to C skipped this tick: sentBatch pending'), and the hook that closes finished exit cases defers too. A withdrawal can also fail when debts recorded against the hub take precedence. So taking earnings out pauses filling and closing until the chain confirms, and nothing in the flow or its end state says so; the cost is paid requests and exit cases waiting longer, with deadlines running, because of the operator's own withdrawal."))
